{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-50143" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/50472b92dc5fbcc8688bb1d5459f910fb2d3f3c0ee564ba671cb6a53ce8df721.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{y}$\n- ${y}^{′}={e}^{y+1}$\n- ${y}^{′}=x(y+1)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/state.json" "file_sha256" "08f9e2476fe2d18ce3a1c0bc8c8b180476d96f389b0fcfa28a8b9d92b380b3a4"} {"question" "q-50143" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/e328914d55b82fdd07147f683aa44aebbc3f3e908f180e6632332119c81ce482.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the line $y=-1,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on this line. \n\nWith that in mind, let's check each equation:\n\n- Equation I does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{y}$ is never zero.\n\n- Equation II does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}={e}^{y+1}$ is never zero.\n\n- Equation III does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=x(y+1)$ evaluates to zero at $x=0$ and $y=-1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of those listed.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/state.json" "file_sha256" "08f9e2476fe2d18ce3a1c0bc8c8b180476d96f389b0fcfa28a8b9d92b380b3a4"} {"question" "q-50143" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6a5f84052723c752ab0d5969f3f4d5246e708864ef8b62ada4ea3a9cf9508e9a"} {"question" "q-50143" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/state.json" "file_sha256" "08f9e2476fe2d18ce3a1c0bc8c8b180476d96f389b0fcfa28a8b9d92b380b3a4"} {"question" "q-50143" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "None of those listed"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/state.json" "file_sha256" "08f9e2476fe2d18ce3a1c0bc8c8b180476d96f389b0fcfa28a8b9d92b380b3a4"} {"question" "q-50143" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "None of those listed"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/state.json" "file_sha256" "08f9e2476fe2d18ce3a1c0bc8c8b180476d96f389b0fcfa28a8b9d92b380b3a4"} {"question" "q-120228" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/204be6af825ef678e06526bff1fdac14ca5f09a2a7bee939620f3f2be1496985.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=x(3-y)$\n- ${y}^{′}=(x+3)y$\n- ${y}^{′}=(3-x)y$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/state.json" "file_sha256" "08f9e2476fe2d18ce3a1c0bc8c8b180476d96f389b0fcfa28a8b9d92b380b3a4"} {"question" "q-120228" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/9051213882bcb506ddae46e0a6a79c8cd03a10eadd89e0007f895f68b00c33de.png)\n\nNotice that the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=3$ and $y=0,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on these lines. \n\nWith that in mind, let's check each equation:\n\n- Equation I does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=x(3-y)$ evaluates to zero at $x=0$ and $y=3.$\n\n- Equation II does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=(x+3)y$ evaluates to zero at $x=-3$ and $y=0.$\n\n- Equation III gives the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=(3-x)y$ evaluates to zero at $x=3$ and $y=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/state.json" "file_sha256" "08f9e2476fe2d18ce3a1c0bc8c8b180476d96f389b0fcfa28a8b9d92b380b3a4"} {"question" "q-120228" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6a5f84052723c752ab0d5969f3f4d5246e708864ef8b62ada4ea3a9cf9508e9a"} {"question" "q-120228" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/state.json" "file_sha256" "08f9e2476fe2d18ce3a1c0bc8c8b180476d96f389b0fcfa28a8b9d92b380b3a4"} {"question" "q-120228" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/state.json" "file_sha256" "08f9e2476fe2d18ce3a1c0bc8c8b180476d96f389b0fcfa28a8b9d92b380b3a4"} {"question" "q-120228" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/state.json" "file_sha256" "08f9e2476fe2d18ce3a1c0bc8c8b180476d96f389b0fcfa28a8b9d92b380b3a4"} {"question" "q-50108" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/fb373420ddaa81aa41e85145dce11d71068740e8039522c5d68d861de606bec3.png)\n\nThe differential equation that generates the slope field shown above is given by\n${y}^{′}=(x-a)(y-b).$\nFind the value of $a+b.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/state.json" "file_sha256" "08f9e2476fe2d18ce3a1c0bc8c8b180476d96f389b0fcfa28a8b9d92b380b3a4"} {"question" "q-50108" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/e9011b88061abae826e12742ccd47a272bef9e969f204cd7e8ca63edf908a7f8.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=2$ and $y=2,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines. \n\nSo, our differential equation must be\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=(x-2)(y-2).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $a=2,$ $b=2,$ and $a+b=4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/state.json" "file_sha256" "08f9e2476fe2d18ce3a1c0bc8c8b180476d96f389b0fcfa28a8b9d92b380b3a4"} {"question" "q-50108" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6a5f84052723c752ab0d5969f3f4d5246e708864ef8b62ada4ea3a9cf9508e9a"} {"question" "q-50108" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/state.json" "file_sha256" "08f9e2476fe2d18ce3a1c0bc8c8b180476d96f389b0fcfa28a8b9d92b380b3a4"} {"question" "q-50108" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "4"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/state.json" "file_sha256" "08f9e2476fe2d18ce3a1c0bc8c8b180476d96f389b0fcfa28a8b9d92b380b3a4"} {"question" "q-50108" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/state.json" "file_sha256" "08f9e2476fe2d18ce3a1c0bc8c8b180476d96f389b0fcfa28a8b9d92b380b3a4"} {"question" "q-50107" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/bd48f9adcb39961a1ea30314566857bcfb92889b313453bfe76a1aa5405a9e7e.png)\n\nThe differential equation that generates the slope field shown above is given by ${y}^{′}=(x-a)(y-b).$ Find the value of $b-a.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/state.json" "file_sha256" "08f9e2476fe2d18ce3a1c0bc8c8b180476d96f389b0fcfa28a8b9d92b380b3a4"} {"question" "q-50107" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/a7df5da44a77085b7a89685aa80220a8ae4879cb55c350dba294a5b2e281cac9.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=-1$ and $y=-2,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines. \n\nSo, our differential equation must be\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=(x+1)(y+2).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $a=-1,$ $b=-2,$ and $b-a=-1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/state.json" "file_sha256" "08f9e2476fe2d18ce3a1c0bc8c8b180476d96f389b0fcfa28a8b9d92b380b3a4"} {"question" "q-50107" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6a5f84052723c752ab0d5969f3f4d5246e708864ef8b62ada4ea3a9cf9508e9a"} {"question" "q-50107" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/state.json" "file_sha256" "08f9e2476fe2d18ce3a1c0bc8c8b180476d96f389b0fcfa28a8b9d92b380b3a4"} {"question" "q-50107" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-3"] ["math" "-4"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "6"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/state.json" "file_sha256" "08f9e2476fe2d18ce3a1c0bc8c8b180476d96f389b0fcfa28a8b9d92b380b3a4"} {"question" "q-50107" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/state.json" "file_sha256" "08f9e2476fe2d18ce3a1c0bc8c8b180476d96f389b0fcfa28a8b9d92b380b3a4"} {"question" "q-50110" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/d714de862ed316ead00880607077c9090c54cc7c63adc849b5e83190118afec2.png)\n\nThe differential equation that generates the slope field shown above is given by ${y}^{′}=(x-a)(y-b).$ Find the value of $2a-b.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/state.json" "file_sha256" "08f9e2476fe2d18ce3a1c0bc8c8b180476d96f389b0fcfa28a8b9d92b380b3a4"} {"question" "q-50110" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "From the slope field, we see that the slope is zero whenever $x=-3$ or $y=3.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/10ae8ed1fe49b32938dba062ad13e39ce1339c6ac9b94d67f679ab60b288be00.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=-3$ and $y=3,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines. \n\nSo, our differential equation must be\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=(x+3)(y-3).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $a=-3,$ $b=3,$ and $2a-b=2(-3)-3=-9.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/state.json" "file_sha256" "08f9e2476fe2d18ce3a1c0bc8c8b180476d96f389b0fcfa28a8b9d92b380b3a4"} {"question" "q-50110" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6a5f84052723c752ab0d5969f3f4d5246e708864ef8b62ada4ea3a9cf9508e9a"} {"question" "q-50110" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/state.json" "file_sha256" "08f9e2476fe2d18ce3a1c0bc8c8b180476d96f389b0fcfa28a8b9d92b380b3a4"} {"question" "q-50110" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-3"] ["math" "-6"] ["math" "-9"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "6"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/state.json" "file_sha256" "08f9e2476fe2d18ce3a1c0bc8c8b180476d96f389b0fcfa28a8b9d92b380b3a4"} {"question" "q-50110" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-9"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/state.json" "file_sha256" "08f9e2476fe2d18ce3a1c0bc8c8b180476d96f389b0fcfa28a8b9d92b380b3a4"} {"question" "q-117982" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/fe5c82553a127e5b7690d13114e7f06cae20eaea83e0a4d62b994bb4c64a27d6.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/state.json" "file_sha256" "08f9e2476fe2d18ce3a1c0bc8c8b180476d96f389b0fcfa28a8b9d92b380b3a4"} {"question" "q-117982" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/e463bb775deea7ce2f2840d6c3554fffbb93483c6c8694933e78c3c89cca9711.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=-1$ and $y=1,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines. \n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(x+1)^{2}(1-y) \\\\ {y}^{′} & =(x+1)(y-1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, the lines $x=-1$ and $y=1$ divide the plane into $4$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $4$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| $y>1$ | $↘$\n\n$(-)$ | $↘$\n\n$(-)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<1$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ |\n|  | $x<-1$ | $x>-1$ |\n\nOf the remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=(x+1)^{2}(1-y).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/state.json" "file_sha256" "08f9e2476fe2d18ce3a1c0bc8c8b180476d96f389b0fcfa28a8b9d92b380b3a4"} {"question" "q-117982" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6a5f84052723c752ab0d5969f3f4d5246e708864ef8b62ada4ea3a9cf9508e9a"} {"question" "q-117982" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/state.json" "file_sha256" "08f9e2476fe2d18ce3a1c0bc8c8b180476d96f389b0fcfa28a8b9d92b380b3a4"} {"question" "q-117982" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{y}^{′}=(1-x)y"] ["math" "{y}^{′}=(x+1)(y-1)^{2}"] ["math" "{y}^{′}=(x+1)^{2}(1-y)"] ["math" "{y}^{′}=(x+1)^{2}(y+1)"] ["math" "{y}^{′}={x}^{2}(1-y)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/state.json" "file_sha256" "08f9e2476fe2d18ce3a1c0bc8c8b180476d96f389b0fcfa28a8b9d92b380b3a4"} {"question" "q-117982" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{y}^{′}=(x+1)^{2}(1-y)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/state.json" "file_sha256" "08f9e2476fe2d18ce3a1c0bc8c8b180476d96f389b0fcfa28a8b9d92b380b3a4"} {"question" "q-120232" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/be758c93cb1e04380de9b57103c302623400342946996030b22ae12046488172.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=x(3-y)$\n- ${y}^{′}={x}^{2}(y-3)$\n- ${y}^{′}=x(y-3)^{2}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/state.json" "file_sha256" "08f9e2476fe2d18ce3a1c0bc8c8b180476d96f389b0fcfa28a8b9d92b380b3a4"} {"question" "q-120232" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/3afe29e9dd507cd9aae4035ef3bde2f3f2c1865cd258c4d218ec1934d8626a6e.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=0$ and $y=3,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\n- Second, the lines $x=0$ and $y=3$ divide the plane into $4$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $4$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| $y>3$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ | $↘$\n\n$(-)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<3$ | $↘$\n\n$(-)$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ |\n|  | $x<0$ | $x>0$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- Equation I gives the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=x(3-y)$ evaluates to zero at $x=0$ and $y=3,$ and the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| $y>3$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<3$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n|  | $x<0$ | $x>0$ |\n\n- Equation II does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}={x}^{2}(y-3)$ evaluates to zero at $x=0$ and $y=3,$ but the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) do not correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| $y>3$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<3$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n|  | $x<0$ | $x>0$ |\n\n- Equation III does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=x(y-3)^{2}$ evaluates to zero at $x=0$ and $y=3,$ but the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) do not correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| $y>3$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<3$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n|  | $x<0$ | $x>0$ |\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/state.json" "file_sha256" "08f9e2476fe2d18ce3a1c0bc8c8b180476d96f389b0fcfa28a8b9d92b380b3a4"} {"question" "q-120232" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6a5f84052723c752ab0d5969f3f4d5246e708864ef8b62ada4ea3a9cf9508e9a"} {"question" "q-120232" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/state.json" "file_sha256" "08f9e2476fe2d18ce3a1c0bc8c8b180476d96f389b0fcfa28a8b9d92b380b3a4"} {"question" "q-120232" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/state.json" "file_sha256" "08f9e2476fe2d18ce3a1c0bc8c8b180476d96f389b0fcfa28a8b9d92b380b3a4"} {"question" "q-120232" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/state.json" "file_sha256" "08f9e2476fe2d18ce3a1c0bc8c8b180476d96f389b0fcfa28a8b9d92b380b3a4"} {"question" "q-50160" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/50e6b2cca3b08d4b98d1a7be174816cd8e3d726b4e9af29e6605bf365a474eb3.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/state.json" "file_sha256" "08f9e2476fe2d18ce3a1c0bc8c8b180476d96f389b0fcfa28a8b9d92b380b3a4"} {"question" "q-50160" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/0b09389629bdd17043a57b2e39551543475e2d19772b41d7b74d3293f4c91aa6.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=2$ and $y=2,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines. \n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(x-2)(y-2)^{2} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =(x-2)(y-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, the lines $x=2$ and $y=2$ divide the plane into $4$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $4$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| $y>2$ | $↘$\n\n$(-)$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<2$ | $↘$\n\n$(-)$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ |\n|  | $x<2$ | $x>2$ |\n\nOf the remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=(x-2)(y-2)^{2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/state.json" "file_sha256" "08f9e2476fe2d18ce3a1c0bc8c8b180476d96f389b0fcfa28a8b9d92b380b3a4"} {"question" "q-50160" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6a5f84052723c752ab0d5969f3f4d5246e708864ef8b62ada4ea3a9c