on-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/e337a67c316dbee9e9c73849f363a7b2c3e3cb485bfef8eb49e87c3e33b98cf1.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=2$ and $y=0,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\n- Second, the lines $x=2$ and $y=0$ divide the plane into $4$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $4$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| $y>0$ | $↘$\n\n$(-)$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<0$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ | $↘$\n\n$(-)$ |\n|  | $x<2$ | $x>2$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- Equation I does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=x(y-2)^{2}$ evaluates to zero at $x=0$ and $y=2.$\n\n- Equation II does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=(x-2)^{2}y$ evaluates to zero at $x=2$ and $y=0,$ but the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) do not correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| $y>0$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<0$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n|  | $x<2$ | $x>2$ |\n\n- Equation III gives the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=(x-2)y$ evaluates to zero at $x=2$ and $y=0,$ and the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| $y>0$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<0$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n|  | $x<2$ | $x>2$ |\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/state.json" "file_sha256" "08f9e2476fe2d18ce3a1c0bc8c8b180476d96f389b0fcfa28a8b9d92b380b3a4"} {"question" "q-120235" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value"