capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/state.json" "file_sha256" "08f9e2476fe2d18ce3a1c0bc8c8b180476d96f389b0fcfa28a8b9d92b380b3a4"} {"question" "q-120264" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/7d0d7ff6fa9c7cd074fcc2321f125ac5da8142e55578459f44903555e2797204.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=4$ and $y=0,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\n- Second, the lines $x=4$ and $y=0$ divide the plane into $4$ quarters. The sign of the slopes in each of the $4$ quarters are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| $y>0$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ | $↘$\n\n$(-)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<0$ | $↘$\n\n$(-)$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ |\n|  | $x<4$ | $x>4$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- Equation I does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=4-y\n$$\n\nevaluates to zero at $y=4$ only.\n\n- Equation II gives the required behavior. The right-hand side of\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=8y-2xy=2y(4-x)\n$$\n\nevaluates to zero at $x=4$ and $y=0,$ and the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| $y>0$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<0$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n|  | $x<4$ | $x>4$ |\n\n- Equation III does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}y-8xy+16y=(x-4)^{2}y\n$$\n\nevaluates to zero at $x=4$ and $y=0,$ but the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) do not correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| $y>0$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<0$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n|  | $x<4$ | $x>4$ |\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/state.json" "file_sha256" "08f9e2476fe2d18ce3a1c0bc8c8b180476d96f389b0fcfa28a8b9d92b380b3a4"} {"question" "q-120264" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6a5f84052723c752ab0d5969f3f4d5246e708864ef8b62ada4ea3a9cf9508e9a"} {"question" "q-120264" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/state.json" "file_sha256" "08f9e2476fe2d18ce3a1c0bc8c8b180476d96f389b0fcfa28a8b9d92b380b3a4"} {"question" "q-120264" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/state.json" "file_sha256" "08f9e2476fe2d18ce3a1c0bc8c8b180476d96f389b0fcfa28a8b9d92b380b3a4"} {"question" "q-120264" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/state.json" "file_sha256" "08f9e2476fe2d18ce3a1c0bc8c8b180476d96f389b0fcfa28a8b9d92b380b3a4"} {"question" "q-120271" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/85fcea0ad26f7da26189c7611a851bfe228d3ad5d428dcba6c8299c8304540d1.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=(x-1){y}^{2}-x+1$\n- ${y}^{′}=xy-y-(x-1)$\n- ${y}^{′}=y-1-x(y-1)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/state.json" "file_sha256" "08f9e2476fe2d18ce3a1c0bc8c8b180476d96f389b0fcfa28a8b9d92b380b3a4"} {"question" "q-120271" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/dc75845932358d060d5878a30f00fa01e2b7b54408bca308110a53ea654ae18c.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=1$ and $y=1,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\n- Second, the lines $x=1$ and $y=1$ divide the plane into $4$ quarters. The sign of the slopes in each of the $4$ quarters are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| $y>1$ | $↘$\n\n$(-)$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<1$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ | $↘$\n\n$(-)$ |\n|  | $x<1$ | $x>1$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- Equation I does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=(x-1){y}^{2}-x+1=(x-1)(y+1)(y-1)\n$$\n\nevaluates to zero at $x=1$ and $y=\\pm 1.$\n\n- Equation II gives the required behavior. The right-hand side of\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=xy-y-(x-1)=(x-1)(y-1)\n$$\n\nevaluates to zero at $x=1$ and $y=1,$ and the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| $y>1$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<1$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n|  | $x<1$ | $x>1$ |\n\n- Equation III does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=y-1-x(y-1)=(1-x)(y-1)\n$$\n\nevaluates to zero at $x=1$ and $y=1,$ but the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) do not correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| $y>1$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<1$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n|  | $x<1$ | $x>1$ |\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/state.json" "file_sha256" "08f9e2476fe2d18ce3a1c0bc8c8b180476d96f389b0fcfa28a8b9d92b380b3a4"} {"question" "q-120271" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6a5f84052723c752ab0d5969f3f4d5246e708864ef8b62ada4ea3a9cf9508e9a"} {"question" "q-120271" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/state.json" "file_sha256" "08f9e2476fe2d18ce3a1c0bc8c8b180476d96f389b0fcfa28a8b9d92b380b3a4"} {"question" "q-120271" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/state.json" "file_sha256" "08f9e2476fe2d18ce3a1c0bc8c8b180476d96f389b0fcfa28a8b9d92b380b3a4"} {"question" "q-120271" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/state.json" "file_sha256" "08f9e2476fe2d18ce3a1c0bc8c8b180476d96f389b0fcfa28a8b9d92b380b3a4"} {"question" "q-118025" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/5077a94fe8b5d5435b5bf50d0ea687f5bc87ae95011e22800ccfe473f8af78dd.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?\n\n- ${y}^{′}={x}^{2}y+{x}^{2}$\n- ${y}^{′}=xy-x$\n- ${y}^{′}=x{y}^{2}+2xy+x$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/state.json" "file_sha256" "08f9e2476fe2d18ce3a1c0bc8c8b180476d96f389b0fcfa28a8b9d92b380b3a4"} {"question" "q-118025" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/2f7075161363dee2817aeb8aff9e85781a10bd30c926bfff73724992eee699ac.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=0$ and $y=-1,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\n- Second, the lines $x=0$ and $y=-1$ divide the plane into $4$ quarters. The sign of the slopes in each of the $4$ quarters are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| $y>-1$ | $↘$\n\n$(-)$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<-1$ | $↘$\n\n$(-)$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ |\n|  | $x<0$ | $x>0$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- Equation I does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}y+{x}^{2}={x}^{2}(y+1)\n$$\n\nevaluates to zero at $x=0$ and $y=-1,$ but the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) do not correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| $y>-1$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<-1$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n|  | $x<0$ | $x>0$ |\n\n- Equation II does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=xy-x=x(y-1)\n$$\n\nevaluates to zero at $x=0$ and $y=1.$\n\n- Equation III gives the required behavior. The right-hand side of\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x{y}^{2}+2xy+x=x(y+1)^{2}\n$$\n\nevaluates to zero at $x=0$ and $y=-1,$ and the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| $y>-1$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<-1$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n|  | $x<0$ | $x>0$ |\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/state.json" "file_sha256" "08f9e2476fe2d18ce3a1c0bc8c8b180476d96f389b0fcfa28a8b9d92b380b3a4"} {"question" "q-118025" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6a5f84052723c752ab0d5969f3f4d5246e708864ef8b62ada4ea3a9cf9508e9a"} {"question" "q-118025" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/state.json" "file_sha256" "08f9e2476fe2d18ce3a1c0bc8c8b180476d96f389b0fcfa28a8b9d92b380b3a4"} {"question" "q-118025" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None of those listed"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/state.json" "file_sha256" "08f9e2476fe2d18ce3a1c0bc8c8b180476d96f389b0fcfa28a8b9d92b380b3a4"} {"question" "q-118025" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/state.json" "file_sha256" "08f9e2476fe2d18ce3a1c0bc8c8b180476d96f389b0fcfa28a8b9d92b380b3a4"} {"question" "q-50187" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/ac08ec40d4aeae745d9aafd2696fc5da7977aa9d01c2766798ac546b829cb112.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/state.json" "file_sha256" "08f9e2476fe2d18ce3a1c0bc8c8b180476d96f389b0fcfa28a8b9d92b380b3a4"} {"question" "q-50187" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/2e9bd658eb426a7e2d5869f2fd9499e846ab82f25edcef6dc5c6f42e38b188bd.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=-1$ and $y=0,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines. \n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =xy+y=(x+1)y \\\\ {y}^{′} & =(x+1)^{2}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, the lines $x=-1$ and $y=0$ divide the plane into $4$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $4$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| $y>0$ | $↘$\n\n$(-)$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<0$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ | $↘$\n\n$(-)$ |\n|  | $x<-1$ | $x>-1$ |\n\n \nOf the remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=xy+y=(x+1)y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}=xy+y.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/state.json" "file_sha256" "08f9e2476fe2d18ce3a1c0bc8c8b180476d96f389b0fcfa28a8b9d92b380b3a4"} {"question" "q-50187" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6a5f84052723c752ab0d5969f3f4d5246e708864ef8b62ada4ea3a9cf9508e9a"} {"question" "q-50187" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/state.json" "file_sha256" "08f9e2476fe2d18ce3a1c0bc8c8b180476d96f389b0fcfa28a8b9d92b380b3a4"} {"question" "q-50187" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{y}^{′}=(x+1)(y+2)"] ["math" "{y}^{′}=(x+1)^{2}y"] ["math" "{y}^{′}=xy+y"] ["math" "{y}^{′}={y}^{2}+x"] ["math" "{y}^{′}={y}^{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/state.json" "file_sha256" "08f9e2476fe2d18ce3a1c0bc8c8b180476d96f389b0fcfa28a8b9d92b380b3a4"} {"question" "q-50187" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{y}^{′}=xy+y"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/state.json" "file_sha256" "08f9e2476fe2d18ce3a1c0bc8c8b180476d96f389b0fcfa28a8b9d92b380b3a4"} {"question" "q-120266" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/26a0a41cc50b6d5cb586c52e6a4e6bd5bd324f3e13ad7eef4ee0d9eed18340ea.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=2y-xy$\n- ${y}^{′}={x}^{2}{y}^{2}+4x{y}^{2}+4{y}^{2}$\n- ${y}^{′}=x{y}^{2}-2{y}^{2}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/state.json" "file_sha256" "08f9e2476fe2d18ce3a1c0bc8c8b180476d96f389b0fcfa28a8b9d92b380b3a4"} {"question" "q-120266" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/249aba327a8103645963ce95e7935019a279b4cf0d9306111c41172ae22f792b.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=2$ and $y=0,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\n- Second, the lines $x=2$ and $y=0$ divide the plane into $4$ quarters. The sign of the slopes in each of the $4$ quarters are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| $y>0$ | $↘$\n\n$(-)$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<0$ | $↘$\n\n$(-)$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ |\n|  | $x<2$ | $x>2$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- Equation I does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2y-xy=(2-x)y\n$$\n\nevaluates to zero at $x=2$ and $y=0,$ but the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) do not correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| $y>0$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<0$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n|  | $x<2$ | $x>2$ |\n\n- Equation II does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}{y}^{2}+4x{y}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=(x+2)^{2}{y}^{2}\n$$\n\nevaluates to zero at $x=-2$ and $y=0.$\n\n- Equation III gives the required behavior. The right-hand side of\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x{y}^{2}-2{y}^{2}=(x-2){y}^{2}\n$$\n\nevaluates to zero at $x=2$ and $y=0,$ and the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| $y>0$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<0$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n|  | $x<2$ | $x>2$ |\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/state.json" "file_sha256" "08f9e2476fe2d18ce3a1c0bc8c8b180476d96f389b0fcfa28a8b9d92b380b3a4"} {"question" "q-120266" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6a5f84052723c752ab0d5969f3f4d5246e708864ef8b62ada4ea3a9cf9508e9a"} {"question" "q-120266" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/state.json" "file_sha256" "08f9e2476fe2d18ce3a1c0bc8c8b180476d96f389b0fcfa28a8b9d92b380b3a4"} {"question" "q-120266" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/state.json" "file_sha256" "08f9e2476fe2d18ce3a1c0bc8c8b180476d96f389b0fcfa28a8b9d92b380b3a4"} {"question" "q-120266" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/state.json" "file_sha256" "08f9e2476fe2d18ce3a1c0bc8c8b180476d96f389b0fcfa28a8b9d92b380b3a4"} {"question" "q-49940" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/423d393e3ec1f666f384da26ce1df67fd38b977ccad6ff1f56360346ed36d0cc.png)\n\nIdentify the differential equation that generated the slope field above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/state.json" "file_sha256" "08f9e2476fe2d18ce3a1c0bc8c8b180476d96f389b0fcfa28a8b9d92b380b3a4"} {"question" "q-49940" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/423d393e3ec1f666f384da26ce1df67fd38b977ccad6ff1f56360346ed36d0cc.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slope lines on the same vertical line are all the same. This means that the slope does not vary with $y.$ In other words, ${y}^{′}$ is a function of $x$ only.\n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}} \\\\  & {y}^{′}={e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, the slopes become horizontal as $x\\to \\pm \\infty .$ \n\nOf the remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\to 0\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to \\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for the differential equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}$ become horizontal as $x\\to \\pm \\infty .$\nBy contrast, \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={e}^{x}\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/state.json" "file_sha256" "08f9e2476fe2d18ce3a1c0bc8c8b180476d96f389b0fcfa28a8b9d92b380b3a4"} {"question" "q-49940" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6a5f84052723c752ab0d5969f3f4d5246e708864ef8b62ada4ea3a9cf9508e9a"} {"question" "q-49940" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/state.json" "file_sha256" "08f9e2476fe2d18ce3a1c0bc8c8b180476d96f389b0fcfa28a8b9d92b380b3a4"} {"question" "q-49940" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}"] ["math" "{y}^{′}=\\frac{y}{1+x}"] ["math" "{y}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)"] ["math" "{y}^{′}=y-{x}^{2}"] ["math" "{y}^{′}={e}^{x}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/state.json" "file_sha256" "08f9e2476fe2d18ce3a1c0bc8c8b180476d96f389b0fcfa28a8b9d92b380b3a4"} {"question" "q-49940" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/state.json" "file_sha256" "08f9e2476fe2d18ce3a1c0bc8c8b180476d96f389b0fcfa28a8b9d92b380b3a4"} {"question" "q-50347" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/6f3fa328fce3347548661ec19a4a7bcd628df59d603885445c215bcf7e23eee5.png)\n\nIdentify the differential equation that generated the slope field above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/state.json" "file_sha256" "08f9e2476fe2d18ce3a1c0bc8c8b180476d96f389b0fcfa28a8b9d92b380b3a4"} {"question" "q-50347" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/78f281b8039a5897ca8013324c676095b6e1f57278cbab35b05c5eb3b4360301.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes become horizontal along the lines $y=\\pm x.$\n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {y}^{′}=\\frac{2({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})}{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & {y}^{′}={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, for $x\\ne 0,$ the slopes become vertical as $y\\to 0.$ \n\nOf the remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{2({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})}{{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFor $x\\ne 0,$ notice that\n\n$$\n|{y}^{′}|=|\\frac{2({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})}{{y}^{2}}|\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,y\\to 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for the differential equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{2({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})}{{y}^{2}}$ become vertical as $y\\to 0.$\nBy contrast, \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}\\to -{x}^{2}\\,\\text{as}\\,y\\to 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{2({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})}{{y}^{2}}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/state.json" "file_sha256" "08f9e2476fe2d18ce3a1c0bc8c8b180476d96f389b0fcfa28a8b9d92b380b3a4"} {"question" "q-50347" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6a5f84052723c752ab0d5969f3f4d5246e708864ef8b62ada4ea3a9cf9508e9a"} {"question" "q-50347" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/state.json" "file_sha256" "08f9e2476fe2d18ce3a1c0bc8c8b180476d96f389b0fcfa28a8b9d92b380b3a4"} {"question" "q-50347" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{y}^{′}=\\frac{2({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})}{{y}^{2}}"] ["math" "{y}^{′}=\\frac{2x}{y}"] ["math" "{y}^{′}=\\frac{x+y}{y}"] ["math" "{y}^{′}=xy"] ["math" "{y}^{′}={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/state.json" "file_sha256" "08f9e2476fe2d18ce3a1c0bc8c8b180476d96f389b0fcfa28a8b9d92b380b3a4"} {"question" "q-50347" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{y}^{′}=\\frac{2({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})}{{y}^{2}}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/state.json" "file_sha256" "08f9e2476fe2d18ce3a1c0bc8c8b180476d96f389b0fcfa28a8b9d92b380b3a4"} {"question" "q-80714" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/fd7e9afcccbbb635d6bde139697aec0cbaa4bad486ea8b098e96f74bfbefebc9.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{y}$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x}$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{2}{y-1}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/state.json" "file_sha256" "08f9e2476fe2d18ce3a1c0bc8c8b180476d96f389b0fcfa28a8b9d92b380b3a4"} {"question" "q-80714" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/28c4751694caf50f08f73d7ccf3525877e6679901d49854951810f550a65ede2.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slope lines on the same horizontal line are all the same. This means that the slope does not vary with $x.$ In other words, ${y}^{′}$ is a function of $y$ only.\n\n- Second, the slopes become vertical as $y\\to 1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- Equation I does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{y}$ is a function of $y$ only, but\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{y}\\to 1\\,\\text{as}\\,y\\to 1.\n$$\n\n- Equation II does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x}$ is a function of $x$ only.\n\n- Equation III gives the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=\\frac{2}{y-1}$ is a function of $y$ only, and \n\n$$\n|{y}^{′}|=|\\frac{2}{y-1}|\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,y\\to 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/state.json" "file_sha256" "08f9e2476fe2d18ce3a1c0bc8c8b180476d96f389b0fcfa28a8b9d92b380b3a4"} {"question" "q-80714" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6a5f84052723c752ab0d5969f3f4d5246e708864ef8b62ada4ea3a9cf9508e9a"} {"question" "q-80714" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/state.json" "file_sha256" "08f9e2476fe2d18ce3a1c0bc8c8b180476d96f389b0fcfa28a8b9d92b380b3a4"} {"question" "q-80714" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/state.json" "file_sha256" "08f9e2476fe2d18ce3a1c0bc8c8b180476d96f389b0fcfa28a8b9d92b380b3a4"} {"question" "q-80714" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/state.json" "file_sha256" "08f9e2476fe2d18ce3a1c0bc8c8b180476d96f389b0fcfa28a8b9d92b380b3a4"} {"question" "q-50349" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/6a64064f114ac2e6acafa791c4fda50849cad6f3387c4793bdf991c95ae8e542.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{x}{y+1}$\n- ${y}^{′}=x+y$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{x}{y-1}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/state.json" "file_sha256" "08f9e2476fe2d18ce3a1c0bc8c8b180476d96f389b0fcfa28a8b9d92b380b3a4"} {"question" "q-50349" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/6a119b24c773a25bf3530dbfb8574c710cc37a5e970d92112aee5f489551ef02.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become horizontal at $x=0.$\n\n- For $x\\ne 0,$ the slopes of the slope field become vertical as $y\\to -1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- Equation I gives the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=\\frac{x}{y+1}$ evaluates to zero on $x=0,$ and for $x\\ne 0,$ we have \n\n$$\n|{y}^{′}|=|\\frac{x}{y+1}|\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,y\\to -1.\n$$\n\n- Equation II does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=x+y$ does not evaluate to zero on $x=0$ (the value depends on $y$).\n\n- Equation III does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=\\frac{x}{y-1}$ evaluates to zero on $x=0,$ but for $x\\ne 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=|\\frac{x}{y-1}|\\to \\frac{|x|}{2}\\,\\text{as}\\,y\\to -1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/state.json" "file_sha256" "08f9e2476fe2d18ce3a1c0bc8c8b180476d96f389b0fcfa28a8b9d92b380b3a4"} {"question" "q-50349" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6a5f84052723c752ab0d5969f3f4d5246e708864ef8b62ada4ea3a9cf9508e9a"} {"question" "q-50349" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/state.json" "file_sha256" "08f9e2476fe2d18ce3a1c0bc8c8b180476d96f389b0fcfa28a8b9d92b380b3a4"} {"question" "q-50349" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/state.json" "file_sha256" "08f9e2476fe2d18ce3a1c0bc8c8b180476d96f389b0fcfa28a8b9d92b380b3a4"} {"question" "q-50349" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/state.json" "file_sha256" "08f9e2476fe2d18ce3a1c0bc8c8b180476d96f389b0fcfa28a8b9d92b380b3a4"} {"question" "q-50348" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/097a662a461b31dbcadc4e3fc2d9cb5c439c251792b72c768581055783e35379.png)\n\nIdentify the differential equation that generated the slope field above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/state.json" "file_sha256" "08f9e2476fe2d18ce3a1c0bc8c8b180476d96f389b0fcfa28a8b9d92b380b3a4"} {"question" "q-50348" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/9cee91c4500060ff7b8cb2e35a8748614b9bb7a9b267e2c8ee58d6273a7561f1.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes become horizontal along the lines $y=-x.$\n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {y}^{′}=\\frac{x+y}{x} \\\\  & {y}^{′}=x+y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, for $y\\ne 0,$ the slopes become vertical as $x\\to 0.$ \n\nOf the remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{x+y}{x}.\n$$\n\nFor $y\\ne 0,$ notice that\n\n$$\n|{y}^{′}|=|\\frac{x+y}{x}|\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for the differential equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{x+y}{x}$ become vertical as $x\\to 0.$\nBy contrast, \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x+y\\to y\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{x+y}{x}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/state.json" "file_sha256" "08f9e2476fe2d18ce3a1c0bc8c8b180476d96f389b0fcfa28a8b9d92b380b3a4"} {"question" "q-50348" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6a5f84052723c752ab0d5969f3f4d5246e708864ef8b62ada4ea3a9cf9508e9a"} {"question" "q-50348" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/state.json" "file_sha256" "08f9e2476fe2d18ce3a1c0bc8c8b180476d96f389b0fcfa28a8b9d92b380b3a4"} {"question" "q-50348" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{y}^{′}=\\frac{x+y}{x}"] ["math" "{y}^{′}=\\frac{x-y}{x}"] ["math" "{y}^{′}=\\frac{x}{y}"] ["math" "{y}^{′}=x+y"] ["math" "{y}^{′}=x-y"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/state.json" "file_sha256" "08f9e2476fe2d18ce3a1c0bc8c8b180476d96f389b0fcfa28a8b9d92b380b3a4"} {"question" "q-50348" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{y}^{′}=\\frac{x+y}{x}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/state.json" "file_sha256" "08f9e2476fe2d18ce3a1c0bc8c8b180476d96f389b0fcfa28a8b9d92b380b3a4"} {"question" "e-9356" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/4adbd41200bb3fdcd75f7f19530ca810cb67970b732e897845bfd621fee180b3.png)\n\n \n\nThe differential equation that generates the slope field shown above is given by $y' = (x-a)(y-b).$ Find the value of $a + b.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/example-9356.json" "file_sha256" "dfea0a6f1ee7b979beaba3af84c813bdd211f3a1e321e5774ca11dc12bf174a2"} {"question" "e-9356" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/5401c18589eeafd8ac4139de98fb41879d03c0731938c42b2dae50da629de0ce.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x = -2$ and $y = 2,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines. \n\nSo, our differential equation must be\n\n$$\ny' = (x+2)(y-2).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $a = -2,$ $b = 2,$ and $a + b=0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/example-9356.json" "file_sha256" "dfea0a6f1ee7b979beaba3af84c813bdd211f3a1e321e5774ca11dc12bf174a2"} {"question" "e-7126" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/fc130ae2d3404808ba1cbaa66667979728c535320934bb0dbff463ef467572e4.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- $y' = 2xy$\n\n- $y' = -x(y-1)^2$\n\n- $y' = \\dfrac{1}{2}x^2(y-1)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/example-7126.json" "file_sha256" "6a7160d9f798da50cd6f6fad8a3beb62d5af6d0d58833b36490406b938970226"} {"question" "e-7126" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/0c3b7cc8b39735429b1f39e8421fd280e4687f15c5806af0b102900b5e3f8cd8.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=0$ and $y=1,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\n- Second, the lines $x=0$ and $y=1$ divide the plane into $4$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $4$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| $y > 1$ | $\\nearrow$\n\n$({\\color{blue}{+}})$ | $\\nearrow$\n\n$({\\color{blue}{+}})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y <     1$ | $\\searrow$\n\n$({\\color{red}{-}})$ | $\\searrow$\n\n$({\\color{red}{-}})$ |\n|  | $x<     0$ | $x> 0$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- Equation I does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of $y' = 2xy$ evaluates to zero at $x=0$ and $y=0.$\n\n- Equation II does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of $y' = -x(y-1)^2$ evaluates to zero at $x=0$ and $y=1,$ but the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) do not correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| $y > 1$ | ${\\color{blue}{+}}$ | ${\\color{red}{-}}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y <     1$ | ${\\color{blue}{+}}$ | ${\\color{red}{-}}$ |\n|  | $x<     0$ | $x> 0$ |\n\n- Equation III gives the required behavior. The right-hand side of $y' = \\dfrac{1}{2}x^2(y-1)$ evaluates to zero at $x=0$ and $y=1,$ and the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| $y > 1$ | ${\\color{blue}{+}}$ | ${\\color{blue}{+}}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y <     1$ | ${\\color{red}{-}}$ | ${\\color{red}{-}}$ |\n|  | $x<     0$ | $x> 0$ |\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/example-7126.json" "file_sha256" "6a7160d9f798da50cd6f6fad8a3beb62d5af6d0d58833b36490406b938970226"} {"question" "e-6988" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/3a0ec9ab00e65eb1b63a6b73c81c615d7388617814ba6351b1575a96b732eb4d.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?\n\n- $y' = x^2(y+1)+2(y+1)$\n\n- $y' = x(y+1)+2(y+1)$\n\n- $y' = x(y+1)^2+2(y+1)^2$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/example-6988.json" "file_sha256" "b7b6727eb49d59943e3c60ad90048f05c6a77b05ad71c180a3716cfd7116e98b"} {"question" "e-6988" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/b64005397add7c39b60c49bc708f3ee8cccc2e03786278df22e914fc928998db.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=-2$ and $y=-1,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\n- Second, the lines $x=-2$ and $y=-1$ divide the plane into $4$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $4$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| $y > -1$ | $\\searrow$\n\n$({\\color{red}{-}})$ | $\\nearrow$\n\n$({\\color{blue}{+}})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y <      -1$ | $\\nearrow$\n\n$({\\color{blue}{+}})$ | $\\searrow$\n\n$({\\color{red}{-}})$ |\n|  | $x< -2$ | $x> -2$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- Equation I does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of\n\n$$\ny' = x^2(y+1)+2(y+1) = (x^2 + 2)(y + 1)\n$$\n\nevaluates to zero at $y = -1$ only.\n\n- Equation II gives the required behavior. The right-hand side of\n\n$$\ny' = x(y+1)+2(y+1) = (x+2)(y+1)\n$$\n\nevaluates to zero at $x = -2$ and $y = -1,$ and the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| $y > -1$ | ${\\color{red}{-}}$ | ${\\color{blue}{+}}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y <      -1$ | ${\\color{blue}{+}}$ | ${\\color{red}{-}}$ |\n|  | $x< -2$ | $x> -2$ |\n\n- Equation III does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of\n\n$$\ny' = x(y+1)^2+2(y+1)^2 = (x+2)(y+1)^2\n$$\n\nevaluates to zero at $x=-2$ and $y=-1,$ but the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) do not correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| $y >    -1$ | ${\\color{red}{-}}$ | ${\\color{blue}{+}}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y <   -1$ | ${\\color{red}{-}}$ | ${\\color{blue}{+}}$ |\n|  | $x< -2$ | $x> -2$ |\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is equation II." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/example-6988.json" "file_sha256" "b7b6727eb49d59943e3c60ad90048f05c6a77b05ad71c180a3716cfd7116e98b"} {"question" "e-6986" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/d59da4d56d2d664641b5b52136db98c70c6d8ca3efb6903cc7524f748776486e.png)\n\n \n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- $y' = x + y + 1$\n\n- $y' = \\dfrac{xy}{y+1}$\n\n- $y' = \\dfrac{y}{x+1}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/example-6986.json" "file_sha256" "166ed4aba7217f581a9d2ed084b6664ee958e6838b51439b4a5777d01c9a153d"} {"question" "e-6986" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/a692d04d777a7770bfed51d27e53e4d5a0f19860a22afa598875b64bdd85a25c.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become vertical as $x \\to -1.$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become horizontal at $y=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- Equation I does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of $y' = x + y + 1$ does not evaluate to zero on $y=0$ (the value depends on $x$).\n\n- Equation II does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of $y' = \\dfrac{xy}{y+1}$ evaluates to zero on $y=0,$ but \n\n$$\ny' = \\dfrac{xy}{y+1} \\to -\\dfrac{y}{y+1} \n\\quad \\textrm{as} \\quad \nx \\to -1,\n$$\n\nand hence $y'$ depends on $y$ along the line $x=-1.$\n\n- Equation III gives the required behavior. The right-hand side of $y' = \\dfrac{y}{x+1}$ evaluates to zero on $y=0,$ and \n\n$$\ny' = \\bigg| \\dfrac{y}{x+1} \\bigg| \\to \\infty\n\\quad \\textrm{as} \\quad \nx \\to -1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/example-6986.json" "file_sha256" "166ed4aba7217f581a9d2ed084b6664ee958e6838b51439b4a5777d01c9a153d"}] "usage" {"q-50160" [] "e-6988" [] "q-49940" [] "q-50189" [] "e-9356" [] "e-6986" [] "q-50108" [] "e-7126" []} "basis" 1674 "retractions" [[17592186172494 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3350/Source/Images/q-50108.png)\n\nThe differential equation that generates the slope field shown above is given by\n$y^{′} = (x - a)(y - b)$.\nFind the value of $a + b$."] [17592186172495 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3350/Source/Images/q-50160.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?"] [17592186172497 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3350/Source/Images/q-50189.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n1. $y^{′} = 2xy$\n2. $y^{′} =-xy$\n3. $y^{′} = x + 1$"] [17592186172492 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3350/Source/Images/q-49940.png)\n\nIdentify the differential equation that generated the slope field above."] [17592186171820 :question/problem "The differential equation that generates the slope field shown above is given by $y' = (x-a)(y-b)$. Find the value of $a + b$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3350/Source/Images/fd5112fd081ff4dbdf41ceb7d6b6ccc4.png)"] [17592186171820 :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3350/Source/Images/3327d1834e198b8eab50a19a54e5b9b1.png)\n\nNotice that the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x = -2$ and $y = 2$, as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\nSo, our differential equation must be\n\n$$\ny' = (x+2)(y-2)\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $a = -2$, $b = 2$, and\n\n$$\na + b=0\n$$"] [17592186171753 :question/problem "Which of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n![]