ram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3350/Source/Images/321b6eb66ca267d3844c0960f615026d.png)\n\n1. $y' = x^2(y+1)+2(y+1)$\n2. $y' = x(y+1)+2(y+1)$\n3. $y' = x(y+1)^2+2(y+1)^2$"] [17592186171752 :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3350/Source/Images/e8dd09383c592fafe22b0fd1c05a391a.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=-2$ and $y=-1$, as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n- Second, the lines $x=-2$ and $y=-1$ divide the plane into $4$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $4$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:$y > -1$$\\searrow$\n\n$({\\color{red}{-}})$$\\nearrow$\n\n$({\\color{blue}{+}})$$y < -1$$\\nearrow$\n\n$({\\color{blue}{+}})$$\\searrow$\n\n$({\\color{red}{-}})$$x< -2$ $x> -2$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- Equation I does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of\n$y' = x^2(y+1)+2(y+1) = (x^2 + 2)(y + 1)$\nevaluates to zero at $y = -1$ only.\n- Equation II gives the required behavior. The right-hand side of\n$y' = x(y+1)+2(y+1) = (x+2)(y+1)$\nevaluates to zero at $x = -2$ and $y = -1$, and the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) correspond to the given slope field.$y > -1$${\\color{red}{-}}$${\\color{blue}{+}}$$y < -1$${\\color{blue}{+}}$${\\color{red}{-}}$$x< -2$ $x> -2$\n- Equation III does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of\n$y' = x(y+1)^2+2(y+1)^2 = (x+2)(y+1)^2$\nevaluates to zero at $x=-2$ and $y=-1$, but the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) do not correspond to the given slope field.$y > -1$${\\color{red}{-}}$${\\color{blue}{+}}$$y < -1$${\\color{red}{-}}$${\\color{blue}{+}}$$x< -2$ $x> -2$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is equation II."] [17592186171751 :question/problem "Which of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3350/Source/Images/42556eddb08beba40a46abcc4af06f69.png)\n\n1. $y' = x + y + 1$\n2. $y' = \\dfrac{xy}{y+1}$\n3. $y' = \\dfrac{y}{x+1}$"] [17592186171751 :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3350/Source/Images/c1cf6111a0a453b8105ef790e0748df2.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become vertical as $x \\to -1$.\n- The slopes of the slope field become horizontal at $y=0$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- Equation I does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of $y' = x + y + 1$ does not evaluate to zero on $y=0$ (the value depends on $x$).\n- Equation II does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of $y' = \\dfrac{xy}{y+1}$ evaluates to zero on $y=0$, but\n$y' = \\dfrac{xy}{y+1} \\to -\\dfrac{y}{y+1} \\quad \\textrm{as} \\quad x \\to -1$,\nand hence $y'$ depends on $y$ along the line $x=-1$.\n- Equation III gives the required behavior. The right-hand side of $y' = \\dfrac{y}{x+1}$ evaluates to zero on $y=0$, and\n$y' = \\bigg \\mid \\dfrac{y}{x+1} \\bigg \\mid \\to \\infty \\quad \\textrm{as} \\quad x \\to -1$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""]] "immutable_answers" [17592186170918 17592186170919 17592186170920 17592186170921 17592186170922 17592186170930 17592186170931 17592186170932 17592186170933 17592186170934 17592186170936 17592186170937 17592186170938 17592186170939 17592186170940 17592186170948 17592186170949 17592186170950 17592186170951 17592186170952] "immutable_fields" [[17592186170917 :answer-field/correct] [17592186170917 :answer-field/id] [17592186170917 :answer-field/key] [17592186170917 :answ